00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.080
Were you, not a good kid,

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:29.580
but were you one they didn't worry about,

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:30.413
or one they were like,

00:01:30.413 --> 00:01:31.951
"Oh he's gonna try and jump off the roof?"

00:01:31.951 --> 00:01:34.230
- So, here's the deal,

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:36.690
is that I was actually
really well behaved.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:37.523
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:37.523 --> 00:01:39.060
- Whereas Joe and Kevin at times were not.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:39.903
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.233
- Look at that.

00:01:42.233 --> 00:01:43.950
- I don't know, you look
like Dennis the Menace there.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:44.783
I'm just saying.

00:01:44.783 --> 00:01:46.380
- But then there would
be these streaks of,

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.830
I remember my first time ever swimming.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:52.500
We lived in this apartment
complex in Dallas, Texas.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.900
And there was a fence around the pool

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.510
and it was the start of the winter season.

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:59.160
So not only was it cold,

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.110
but also the pool was shut and locked.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.657
Because it was dangerous
for kids to go swimming.

00:02:03.657 --> 00:02:06.360
But for some reason at like
three or four years old

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.369
I just decided it would be a
good idea to like hop the fence

00:02:08.369 --> 00:02:11.160
and just go for a swim.

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.710
And my mom of course, like
ran after me as I just

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:14.940
like climbed over this fence.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.920
And so I would do these things
that were so outta character

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.170
and just like nearly fatal.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.423
And, and-

00:02:20.423 --> 00:02:21.737
- Boys do that.

00:02:21.737 --> 00:02:22.570
- [Nick] Yeah, exactly.

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:23.649
- Yeah.

00:02:23.649 --> 00:02:25.410
- I recognize my mom was very patient

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:27.270
and I'm sure I gave her a lot of scares.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.940
But yeah, other than that
I was pretty well behaved.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:31.503
Apart from nearly drowning.

00:02:31.503 --> 00:02:33.453
- Apart from nearly dying in a pool.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:35.820
Oh my gosh, I can't imagine.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.680
I put my kids in swim lessons
because they were babies.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:38.700
Because I was so worried about that.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:39.960
You heard those horror stories.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:40.793
I feel like you're probably

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:42.840
the most patient dad on planet Earth.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:43.673
Are you?

00:02:43.673 --> 00:02:45.480
- I mean, I try to be.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:46.313
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:46.313 --> 00:02:48.030
- Our dad was very patient.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:49.860
- Well you and John, when I
work with you on the Voice,

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:50.880
I feel like you're both kind of,

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.860
you could both be walking Ambien.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.780
You're both that chill.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.347
They're both that chill-

00:02:56.347 --> 00:02:57.180
- Thank you?

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:58.050
- I know I think because I'm opposite.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:02:59.700
I'm always like (imitating bird)

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:00.720
- [Nick] But John especially.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.220
- John especially, he is the one

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.900
I actually call walking Ambien, yes.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:04.830
- He is so chill.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:06.960
- He is so chill that
I thought he was stoned

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.410
every time I saw him.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.290
Turns out he doesn't even get high.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:11.640
I was like, "what"?

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.400
It was the craziest thing ever
finding that information out.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.900
But I feel like you're
a really patient dad,

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.610
that's gonna serve you well.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:18.870
Because you're gonna need it.

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.540
- Yeah, she hasn't started walking yet,

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:22.650
but I'm sure when she does...

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:23.483
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:23.483 --> 00:03:25.590
- It's gonna be patience
and comfortable shoes.

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.300
Because I'll be running after her.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.010
- 100%, and just getting old

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.270
because you turned 30 last year,

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.130
so you're gonna need those Dr. Scholl's.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:35.040
- Yes. Oh I have insoles
in my shoes right now.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.690
- [Kelly] I have insoles.

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:37.800
- So wait, do you turn 30?

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.399
Did you do a big thing?
Are you excited to be 30?

00:03:40.399 --> 00:03:42.660
- We did a thing, we
went to my favorite place

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:43.493
in the world.

00:03:43.493 --> 00:03:47.328
It's a golf spot in Scottsdale, Arizona.

00:03:47.328 --> 00:03:48.161
- Oh.

00:03:48.161 --> 00:03:49.050
- And had some friends out

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:50.532
and we did a little golf tournament.

00:03:50.532 --> 00:03:51.868
And it was really fun.

00:03:51.868 --> 00:03:53.921
You know, the whole kind of weekend

00:03:53.921 --> 00:03:56.266
was birthday activities.

00:03:56.266 --> 00:03:57.714
But I will say that 30-

00:03:57.714 --> 00:03:58.849
- [Kelly] That's cool.

00:03:58.849 --> 00:03:59.682
- You know, it hits different.

00:03:59.682 --> 00:04:00.970
- Yeah.

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:01.803
- [Nick] It's not like-

00:04:01.803 --> 00:04:02.650
- Wait till 40. (audience laughs)

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:04.860
- Yeah, but it, and I say this knowing

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.510
I sound ridiculous.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:10.800
I go into it knowing full
well that someone older

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.620
than me would say, "Yeah,
that you're ridiculous,

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:14.453
you're only 30."

00:04:14.453 --> 00:04:16.950
But the jump from the hangover at 29

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.440
to the hangover at 30
was very real for me.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.690
- Yeah, the recovery time.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:23.160
- It was like, this is not fun.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:24.000
I don't like this.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.290
- Yeah.

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:26.123
- But I used to be able
to just bounce back

00:04:26.123 --> 00:04:26.956
and I was fine.

00:04:26.956 --> 00:04:29.550
And now it's like I don't
like the way I feel.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:32.280
- Well just so you know
when you get to 40,

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:34.380
literally it's overnight.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:39.079
You get out of bed and you're
like, "why does it hurt?"

00:04:39.079 --> 00:04:41.340
I just got out of bed.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:43.230
I should feel rested,
like what's happening?

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:44.940
Yeah, your actual body...

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:46.170
I don't know, you're kind of in shape.

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:47.389
Maybe it'll be different for you.

00:04:47.389 --> 00:04:48.322
(audience cheers)

00:04:48.322 --> 00:04:50.940
So you and your brothers
